Phase 1-4 summary: `length` is captured before `njs_value_to_integer` calls, which can invoke user `valueOf` callbacks that shrink/detach the array. `start`/`delete` are clamped against the stale `length`, not `array->length`. In the fast path the loop `deleted->start[i] = array->start[n]` reads up to `start+delete` from the (possibly shrunk) array, and the `memcpy(&array->start[start], &args[3], items*sizeof(njs_value_t))` writes at a stale offset when `delta == 0` (no expand happens). Concrete trigger: `a = [1,2,3,...,64]; a.splice({valueOf(){a.length = 0; return 0;}}, {valueOf(){return 64;}}, x)` — start=0, delete clamped to 64 based on stale length while `array->length` is 0, giving 64-element OOB read and an OOB write of one njs_value_t.

No guard re-validates against the current `array->length`; `njs_array_expand` is only called when `delta > 0`.

