Known Ghostscript `search`/`rsearch` issue: the post-match substring size at `r_set_size(op1, count + (!forward ? (size - 1) : 0))` is derived from the loop counter rather than recomputed from the actual match pointer. In the backward (rsearch) case the counter has been decremented for iterations already performed, so the remaining-length computation does not equal `r_size(op1) - (ptr - base) - size`, yielding a string whose declared size extends past the end of the original string buffer. Subsequent PostScript operations reading that string read out of bounds (and can be written to, since attributes are inherited from op1).

Concrete trigger: `(abcde) (a) rsearch` — size=1, r_size(op1)=5, count=4, ptr starts at base+4; loop iterates until ptr==base, decrementing count to 0, match at offset 0; post string set to base+1 with size `count + (size-1)` = 0, while pre string size = 0 — sizes inconsistent with the 4 remaining bytes; with longer patterns e.g. `(aaaa) (aa) rsearch` the computed size exceeds the true remainder, exposing bytes past the buffer.

