Phase analysis: `len = enclen(reg->enc, p)` computes multibyte length from the byte at `p` without bounding by `end`. For a truncated multibyte sequence at the tail of the string, `len` can exceed `end - p`, so `p += len` skips past `end` (loop ends, and `p < end` remainder check fails silently), and more importantly `ONIGENC_MBC_CASE_FOLD`/`onig_node_str_cat(snode, foldp, foldp+fold_len)` and subsequent processing read bytes past `end`. Upstream Oniguruma fixed exactly this by changing to the bounded `enclen(enc, p, end)` form. Guards present (`n < 0`, NULL checks, alt_num threshold) do not constrain `len` against `end`.

Concrete trigger: UTF-8 pattern in case-insensitive mode ending with a lone 0xC3 byte (start of 2-byte sequence) as the last byte of the string node; `enclen` returns 2 while only 1 byte remains, so the fold/copy reads 1 byte past `sn->end`.

