**Phase 1-4 (condensed).** The function writes an mtab entry using caller-supplied `devname`, `mountpoint`, and `fstype`, plus a username-derived option string. `addmntent()` performs no escaping/validation of embedded whitespace or newline characters in `mnt_fsname`, `mnt_dir`, or the option value `user=<name>`. mount.cifs is setuid-root and invoked by unprivileged users, whose supplied share name/mount point (or a username containing `
`) is written verbatim into `/etc/mtab`. Concrete trigger: `mount.cifs //srv/share "/tmp/x 0 0"$'
'"/dev/sda1 / ext4 rw 0 0"` — the injected newline creates an attacker-chosen second mtab line, corrupting the mount table (basis of the historical mount.cifs mtab-corruption issues).

Guards present (setreuid check, sigfillset/sigprocmask checks, lock_mtab, DAC capability toggle, calloc NULL check, strlcat bounded copies) are all effective for memory safety and signal/lock races; none validates or escapes the strings placed into the mtab record. So no memory-safety finding survives, but the missing sanitization of externally controlled data written into a structured privileged file does.

