Phase 1-4 (condensed): The label length is read as an 8-bit value (0-255) and only validated against `entry_length`, not against the size of the stack buffer `text[INDX_LABEL_SIZEMAX + 1]`. In the ORDT2 path, `mobi_getstring_ordt` decodes multi-byte ORDT-mapped characters and can expand each input byte into more output bytes, writing into `text` with only `label_length` as its bound, and it returns the expanded length. That returned `label_length` is then used for `malloc(label_length+1)` and `strncpy(..., text, label_length+1)`, reading past `text` and potentially writing past it inside the decode routine. The guards present (`entry_number >= total_entries_count`, `buf->offset + entry_length >= maxlen`, `label_length > entry_length`) are all INCOMPLETE with respect to `INDX_LABEL_SIZEMAX`: none compares label length to the destination buffer capacity. Concrete trigger: craft an INDX record with an entry whose IDXT offsets give `entry_length` = 0x400, first byte of entry = 255, and an ORDT2 table mapping each byte to multi-byte output, so decoding writes >1001 bytes into `text`.

Additionally, in the second `while (i < ptagx_count)` loop, an early `return MOBI_MALLOC_FAILED` leaves earlier-set state, and the tags array is indexed by `i` bounded by `ptagx_count <= tagx->tags_count`, which is fine. The root issue is the missing label-length bound.

