**Phase 1-4 (condensed).** The tagstack is a fixed array of TAGSTACKSIZE entries in the window; valid indices are 0..w_tagstacklen-1. After `jumpto_tag()` (which can run autocommands, change windows/buffers, and shrink/clear the tag stack), the code re-validates with:

```
if (use_tagstack && tagstackidx > curwin->w_tagstacklen)
    tagstackidx = curwin->w_tagstackidx;
```

and at the end:

```
if (use_tagstack && tagstackidx <= curwin->w_tagstacklen)
    curwin->w_tagstackidx = tagstackidx;
```

Both use `>` / `<=` instead of `>=` / `<`. Since `tagstackidx` is incremented (`++tagstackidx`) before the jump, it can equal `curwin->w_tagstacklen` and be accepted as "valid", storing an index one past the last valid entry into `w_tagstackidx`. On a subsequent `:tag`/`:tnext` (or the loop's `continue` path re-reading `tagstack[tagstackidx].tagname`), that index is used to read/write `tagstack[tagstackidx]`, i.e., an entry beyond the used length (and, if the stack was shrunk by an autocommand, beyond initialized/valid data). Concrete trigger: stack length 1, `:tag foo` sets tagstackidx=1; a `BufReadPost`/`SwapExists` autocommand clears the tag stack (w_tagstacklen becomes 0 or the window changes), yet `1 <= len` fails only for len 0 — with len==1 the stale index 1 is stored, and the next `:tag` reads `tagstack[1].tagname` / writes `tagstack[1].fmark`, out of the used range. The guards were cataloged as INCOMPLETE (off-by-one comparison), so the finding survives Step B.

Also, `tagstackidx` is used to index `tagstack` (the pointer cached at entry) throughout the loop, even though autocommands during `jumpto_tag()`/`find_tags()` (tagfunc) can free/realloc or change `curwin`, making `tagstack` stale — the copy of the name mitigates only part of this.

Root cause: off-by-one in the tag stack index validation (CWE-193) leading to out-of-bounds access (CWE-125/787).

