**Phases 1–4 (condensed):** The function operates without rtnl lock when the qdisc class ops are DOIT_UNLOCKED and the classifier kind is unlocked (`rtnl_held` stays false). In that path `q` is obtained by `__tcf_qdisc_find()` without an rtnl lock held and without taking a qdisc reference. After the potentially long-running, sleeping `tp->ops->change()` call, the code dereferences and writes to `q`:

```
if (err == 0) {
        tfilter_notify(net, skb, n, tp, block, q, parent, fh, ...);
        tfilter_put(tp, fh);
        /* q pointer is NULL for shared blocks */
        if (q)
                q->flags &= ~TCQ_F_CAN_BYPASS;
}
```

A concurrent `RTM_DELQDISC`/qdisc graft on the same ifindex (which only needs rtnl, not the chain/filter locks) can free the Qdisc while this thread runs lock-free, so `q->flags &= ~TCQ_F_CAN_BYPASS` and `tfilter_notify(..., q, ...)` write/read freed memory. The `if (q)` NULL check is not a lifetime guard, so it is INCOMPLETE. The same hazard applies to the `goto replay` path where `q` is used across the unlock.

Concrete trigger: create an sch_htb/ingress-style qdisc whose class ops set `QDISC_CLASS_OPS_DOIT_UNLOCKED` (e.g. mq/ingress path) and add a flower filter (unlocked classifier) with `RTM_NEWTFILTER|NLM_F_CREATE` from CPU A, while CPU B repeatedly does `tc qdisc del dev eth0 root`. CPU A then writes to the freed `struct Qdisc`.

No other guard blocks this path; the finding survives Steps A–E.

