**Phase 1-4 (condensed).** This is nettle's `ecc_256_modp` fast reduction. Inputs: `rp` holding 2*size limbs, reduced in place. Loop runs from `n = 2*size-1` down to `n == p->size`, each iteration folding one high limb using the special form of the P-256 prime. Indexing `rp + n - 4` with n ≥ 4 stays in bounds for size 4.

The security-relevant part is the carry/borrow correction chain:

```
t = mpn_submul_1 (rp + n - 4, p->m, 2, q1);
t += cnd_sub_n (q2, rp + n - 3, p->m, 1);
t += (-q2) & 0xffffffff;
...
cy = cnd_add_n (t, rp + n - 4, p->m, 2);
u0 += cy;
u1 += (u0 < cy);
u1 -= (-t) & 0xffffffff;
```

Here `t` after the accumulation of the submul borrow, the conditional-subtract borrow and the `q2` term can exceed 1, yet it is subsequently used as a *boolean* condition for `cnd_add_n` and in the mask `(-t) & 0xffffffff`. Only a single corrective addition of the modulus is performed, so when more than one borrow must be repaid the candidate remainder is left unreduced/incorrect. The estimate correction step (`t = -(mp_limb_t)(u1 > q0); q1 += t; q2 += t + (q1 < t)`) performs only one adjustment of the quotient digit, so `q1` may still be one too large in the boundary cases; the `assert (q2 < 2)` is compiled out with NDEBUG in release builds and therefore provides no guard.

Concrete trigger: an input where the top limbs make `u1 == u0` (e.g. `u1 = u0 = 0xffffffffffffffff` at `n = 7`), which drives `q1` to its maximal value and produces a double borrow out of the two-limb `mpn_submul_1` plus the `q2` subtraction; the single `cnd_add_n` cannot repay both, leaving `rp[2..3]` and the low limbs holding a value ≥ p (or wrapped below zero).

Phase 2 guard inventory: the only checks are the loop bound (`n >= p->size`, EFFECTIVE for indexing) and the `assert` (INCOMPLETE — disabled in production). No guard covers the multi-borrow case.

Impact: incorrect modular reduction in ECC scalar multiplication yields wrong curve points / wrong shared secrets and, in the classic nettle manifestation, can propagate an out-of-range value into subsequent limb arithmetic. This is an incorrect-computation/improper-carry-handling defect (CWE-682) reached with attacker-supplied field elements.

