Phase 1-4 summary: The code mmaps an attacker-controlled ELF and indexes with unvalidated header fields. `shdr = &elf_map[ehdr->e_shoff]`, `shstrtab = shdr[ehdr->e_shstrndx]` have no bounds check against `st.st_size`. `validated_offset` only guards the first section-name loop; the "Section to segment mapping" and ".dynstr/.dynamic" loops compute `name = elf_map + shstrtab.sh_offset + shdr[i].sh_name` and call `strcmp`/`printf` with no validation. Also `dynstr`/`dynamic` are uninitialized locals used as `shdr[dynamic]` if no such sections exist (concrete: an ELF with no `.dynamic` section leaves `dynamic` indeterminate, then `dyn = &elf_map[shdr[dynamic].sh_offset]` and the loop dereferences arbitrary memory). Also `strcpy(&name[15-6], "[...]")` writes into the mapped file at a name pointer, and `flag[4]` receives `strcpy(flag,"   ")` (fits) but flag2str may extend it.

Concrete trigger: craft a 200-byte ELF with e_ident class = ELFCLASS64, e_shoff = 0x41414141, e_shnum = 1 → `shdr[0]` read far past the 200-byte mapping → SIGSEGV / OOB read. Or a valid ELF stripped of `.dynamic` → `dynamic` uninitialized, `count` from garbage, unbounded OOB reads/writes.

Guards inventory: only `validated_offset(name, elf_map, elf_map+st.st_size)` in the first section loop — INCOMPLETE (does not cover e_shoff, e_shstrndx, e_phoff, later name computations, or dynstr/dynamic indices). Error paths after `fstat`/`mmap` failure also leak fd but that's minor.

